    "user_text": "At the loading arcade I see Ora Pell near the conduit access. I stop short of crowding her and keep my hands in view. \"Ora. If you are willing, I would like a word. Here, on your side of the doorway.\"",
    "storyteller_text": "Niko\u2019s attention cuts past you.\n\nAt the far end of the loading arcade, where the conduit access is recessed beneath a yellow maintenance hood, Ora Pell stands with one shoulder against the jamb. She has changed out of whatever rain gear she wore on the west-conduit leg; her coat is too large through the shoulders and dry only in patches. In one hand she holds a paper cup gone white with steam. A small pleasure, purchased somewhere anonymous, and apparently the only thing in the station she is prepared to let remain simple.\n\nShe hears you. Of course she does.\n\nFor several seconds she does not come closer. The rain drapes the space between the dispatch box and the conduit door in wavering strands, and the freight lifts make their slow, indifferent ascent behind the glass.\n\nNiko does not turn toward her. He keeps his place at the threshold, but his voice is pitched for you alone.\n\n\u201cHer being there doesn\u2019t amend anything I said.\u201d\n\n\u201cI know,\u201d Ora says.\n\nHer answer is flat, not cruel. She lifts the cup, takes a careful sip, then pushes herself off the jamb. She crosses only far enough to stand on the arcade side of the doorway, exactly where you asked. Her gaze touches Niko first, a brief confirmation between people who have survived the usefulness of not knowing too much. Then it settles on you.\n\n\u201cYou get one question,\u201d she says. \u201cNot about the receipt. Not about where I\u2019ve been. Not about where I go after this.\u201d\n\nHer eyes flick to Kessa\u2019s table, to the dead-book sheet under its amber lamp, and back.\n\n\u201cAnd you don\u2019t get to turn seeing me into proof that anybody\u2019s safe. I stopped for tea. That\u2019s all you can honestly say.\u201d\n\nKessa leaves the pen where it rests against the bronze token. Her unread Guild indicator pulses once beneath the overturned clasp and goes dark again.\n\n\u201cNera Voss is still dead,\u201d she says. \u201cYour sentence is still yours.\u201d\n\nOra\u2019s mouth tightens at that, perhaps in recognition, perhaps simply because she has spent enough of the night watching people try to make urgency outrank consequence.\n\n\u201cYou wanted a word,\u201d she tells you. \u201cSpend it carefully.\u201d\n\nRain whispers over the conduit door. Niko remains between the dispatch house and the rest of the arcade, offering neither endorsement nor interference. On the table, the dead-book waits beside the conditional Wickglass receipt: one narrow account of a refused inquiry into isolating carriers through family obligations, purchasable only at the price Kessa named.\n\nOra holds her cup in both hands and waits without yielding an inch of the boundary she has drawn.",
